! CDE-on-Wayland X resources.
!
! The shim publishes the root RESOURCE_MANAGER property for every client, from
! the standard files plus, when it is relayed, the palette dtsession's colour
! server publishes (see config/cde-env.sh).  config/river-init points
! XENVIRONMENT here when the user has no resources of their own, and cde-session
! installs a copy where the shim looks for it regardless of the environment, so
! CDE programs get CDE's defaults without a running X server.
!
! What remains here is what CDE would otherwise supply through its own
! config/$LANG/sys.resources and app-defaults: some font choices, the colour-set
! selection, and per-application resources.

*customization:		-color

! Colours come from CDE's colour server: dtsession publishes the palette as
! *background / *foreground in RESOURCE_MANAGER, the shim relays that property
! between clients (config/cde-env.sh) and merges it after these files, so the
! palette wins on conflicting resources.
!
! *ColorUse chooses which colour set dtsession picks.  CDE's own sys.resources
! sets HIGH_COLOR, which makes it use set 4 of Default.dp (#c6b2a8) on a
! TrueColor display; without it the value is DEFAULT (4), which matches none of
! the XmCO_* cases and silently falls back to MEDIUM_COLOR (#999999 with a white
! foreground).  CDE's generated sys.resources is empty in this build, so the
! setting has to live here.
*ColorUse:		HIGH_COLOR

! The palette the colour server starts with.  ColorPalette is a per-screen
! resource -- the colour server's shells are named after the screen number, and
! it publishes *<screen>*ColorPalette -- so the instance is 0.  Once a palette
! has been chosen in the Style Manager, the palette relayed through the shared
! property store (merged after these files) carries its own *0*ColorPalette and
! wins, so this is only the starting point.  Without it dtsession falls back to
! Default.dp on a colour display.
*0*ColorPalette:	Broica.dp

! No *Background / *Foreground fallback here on purpose.  In CDE the colour
! server's publication is the only source of the desktop colours, and a
! class-level fallback of ours would sit in front of the palette's *background /
! *foreground and mask it (clients painted the fallback, not the palette).  If
! the palette is missing, Motif's own default applies, which is what a CDE
! session without a colour server does too.

! A readable proportional and fixed font; the shim matches these XLFDs through
! fontconfig and never fails to load a font.
*fontList: -*-helvetica-medium-r-normal--14-*-*-*-*-*-iso8859-1
*XmText*fontList: -*-courier-medium-r-normal--14-*-*-*-*-*-iso8859-1

! DtTerm's terminal font must be a *fontset*, not a plain font.  In a
! multibyte locale DtTerm stores wide characters; if Motif hands it a plain
! font it takes the single-font XDrawImageString path and only len/4 glyphs
! appear.  Motif's trailing-colon syntax (as used by CDE's own
! sys.resources: `*userFont: ...:^*XmText*FontList`) selects
! XmFONT_IS_FONTSET, so the widget uses Xwc/Xmb instead.
*userFont: -misc-fixed-medium-r-normal--14-*-*-*-*-*-iso8859-1:
*userBoldFont: -misc-fixed-bold-r-normal--14-*-*-*-*-*-iso8859-1:

! Motif derives the highlight, shadow and select colours from the palette
! background, so nothing is pinned here any more: the old hand-picked values
! (highlightColor == background, a blue selectColor) were what made gadget radio
! indicators invisible and forced a blue selection onto a CDE palette.

! The Style Manager - Font dialog matches the current *systemFont against its
! Dtstyle*SystemFont1..7 list (14-pt Helvetica by default).  Set the system
! font to that same entry so the dialog selects the current size instead of
! reporting that the font in use cannot be found (which also leaves the
! preview empty).
*systemFont: -adobe-helvetica-medium-r-normal--14-*-iso8859-1

! CDE window decorations that the shim maps to CoW/River window hints.
*XmDialogShell.title:		CDE

! The CDE session manager (dtsession) must not start a window manager of its
! own: CoW is the window manager here, and the Front Panel (dtwm) is started
! separately by cde-session.sh.  Without this dtsession forks a second dtwm,
! which fights the contained panel over the Workspace Manager protocol.
Dtsession*wmStartupCommand:	/bin/true

! The compositor + shim round-trip can make a synthetic double-click slower than
! CDE's 500 ms default; allow more so double-click-to-open works.
*multiClickTime:	1500
